00:00:20.946 --> 00:00:23.110
GANG CHEN: OK.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:26.960
Let's have a recap
of last lecture.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:33.580
So what we're doing is
use the Boltzmann equation

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:36.350
under relaxation
time approximation.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.800
So that's the relaxation time.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:46.540
And under this approximation, we
can write and also linearize--

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.780
so we keep the first order--

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.710
of the left-hand side of
the Boltzmann equation

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.750
and replace that f by f0.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.990
That's essentially--
so the distortion

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:00.910
due to the transport.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:03.940
And the f0 is the equilibrium.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:08.650
So if f just equal f0,
there's no transport.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.520
But say when we have
a gradient in f0

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:20.590
and in both real space and
velocity of momentum space,

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:24.370
we have the deviation
from the equilibrium.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:29.950
So this is the
first-order solution

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.590
for the Boltzmann equation
under relaxation time

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.120
or approximation.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:40.090
And using this, we say from
here you can look either

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:45.290
the heat flux, the momentum
flux, and the charge flux.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.010
So you can do all
the flux and gather

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.410
the constituent relations.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.700
We derive the Fourier law.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.870
And in this case, we
took a phonon as example.

00:01:55.870 --> 00:02:00.500
f0 is the distribution function.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.250
But in this case,
the temperature

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.620
become space-dependent.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:07.720
So t is the function of x.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.570
And that we do not have
the momentum dependence,

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:11.770
so we took this out.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.020
And you get f0--

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:19.600
f solution, and then from there
you calculate the heat flux.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.000
And the flux is always--

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:29.320
whatever flux we had is f
itself times the velocity

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:31.130
in that direction.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:32.365
And let's say here.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:41.050
And then you sum up
of all the states

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:44.440
and per unit area so
normalize per volume.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.540
So this is the flux itself.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.300
And of course, if you want
to calculate momentum,

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:53.050
you put the momentum
per quantum state.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:54.910
Or if you calculate
the heat, that's

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:58.090
the energy carried
per quantum state.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:01.570
And next what do
we plan to do is

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:05.540
to calculate the charge
flux, the current.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.530
So we're doing electrons.

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:13.450
And let's say as common in
Fourier law, so for a phonon

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.970
it's t is a function of x.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.790
And when we did the
Newton shear stress law,

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:26.140
we say f0, we replace with the
displaced Boltzmann the Maxwell

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:29.500
distribution, because
the molecule has

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:33.160
a long zero average velocity u.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:39.850
This is an average velocity--
v is the phase space velocity.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:43.120
v itself recover from mass
infinite, plus infinite.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.530
But u is a function
of the space only.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:54.100
That's the average over
all the momentum space.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:58.570
So this case, u is
a function of r.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:01.700
So that's where we
took a derivative.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.180
We again look at
the momentum flux.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:07.750
We got the Newton
shear stress law.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:12.160
And I might just comment,
for example, if you want,

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.270
look at this--

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:17.560
if t is a function of x, you
have both momentum transport

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:21.310
and also, say, the spatial
dependent due to temperature.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:26.590
Temperature could also
drive the mass flow.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:30.940
And this is, in fact, the
coupled heat mass transfer.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:35.570
And we didn't go
into those details.

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:38.140
And if you want to derive--

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:39.670
I think one of
the homework, it's

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:44.860
actually doing the derived
thermal conductivity

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.080
for gas molecules.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.050
And in that case,
you will actually

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:57.960
have to do, say, a mass
balance and heat balance.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:03.330
And you will see more clearly
in the example we're treating.

00:05:03.330 --> 00:05:07.590
And that's actually
for couple the charge

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:11.570
and the heat transfer when
we deal with the electrons.

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:16.410
So at the end of last lecture,
we discussed the electron

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:20.460
transport and we finally
show that the solution

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:24.810
will apply to electron is f0.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:29.430
And then this gradient terms,
the complication for electron

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:35.790
is we not only have spatial
gradient due to the Fermi-Dirac

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:39.810
distribution here,
but also we have

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:42.150
the force applied to electron.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:46.350
And because we're going to
have an electrostatic field,

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:52.120
the field will act a force
under electrostatic field.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:55.020
So we combine all
this and we derive.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:59.490
At the end, we have
three terms, df dx.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.490
Ef Is the chemical potential.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:06.580
And dc dx, which is the
electrostatic potential.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:13.410
This is a I'll comment
where the reference--

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:18.220
and dt dx, where I have
temperature gradient.

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:20.460
So I have those three terms.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:22.260
And the df dEk--

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:26.470
Ek is the kinetic energy.

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:32.160
So this is where I
made some comments.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.330
So you have to understand what's
the chemical potential, what's

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.640
the electrostatic
potential, and what's

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.280
the electrochemical potential.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:50.880
So we said the difference
between Ec and the Fermi level

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.110
is the electrochemical
potential.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:59.850
So here, if this mu is relative
to whatever absolute reference,

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:02.460
so flat reference.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:06.520
And this mu is made of the
electrochemical potential,

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.970
which determine how
many particles I have.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:13.140
So how many particles I
have in the conduction band,

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.900
if you look at this, is Ek.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:19.230
So this is the kinetic
energy relative to the bottom

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.120
of the conduction band--

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:24.450
that's Ek-- minus Ef.

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:26.890
So this Ef relative
to Ec is negative,

00:07:26.890 --> 00:07:28.950
so that's the difference there--

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.410
Ek minus Ef.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:39.100
So this is itself determine
the number of particles

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:40.430
in the conduction band.

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:43.960
So that's consistent with
the chemical potential is

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.960
related to number of particles.

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:52.300
And Ec relative to
the absolute level

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:54.790
is where of the potential
energy of the electron,

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:56.350
the bottom of the
electron, that's

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:58.180
where the potential energy is.

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:04.330
So mu, which is a
combination of Ef plus Ec,

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:08.860
relative to absolute, is the
electrochemical potential,

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:10.660
is the combined
chemical potential

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:12.770
with the electric potential.

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:17.470
So when I look at the driving
force, in fact, you can say Ef

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:20.830
and the two gradient,
electrostatic potential

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:23.960
gradient and chemical
potential gradients,

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:26.090
are equivalent positions.

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:29.960
They can both drive
the charge flow.

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:33.080
And then we have
temperature gradient.

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:36.360
So with this, you
can move to rest.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:40.549
For example, I want to
calculate the charge flux.

00:08:40.549 --> 00:08:42.409
I'm doing q.

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:47.390
For electron q is negative
E. For host, q is positive E.

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:51.560
So I'm just going to use q.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:56.660
If I want to look at the x
direction, so I have way vx,

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.140
I have q, and I have f.

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:05.120
I have, say, 2, factor
of 2, count for the spin.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:09.100
And I sum of kx, ky, kz.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:20.300
So you can say, if I replace
q by mass per charge,

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.190
I get the mass flux.

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:25.430
It doesn't matter when I
deal with electron flow,

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:29.870
but it matters when I deal
with the mass diffusion.

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:34.490
So all those is
under the same idea.

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:38.780
Really difference is what's
the quantity we're interested

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:44.510
and what's the detail
of the f itself.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:47.690
And starting from
here, your task

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:52.400
is the same as we did
for the Fourier law,

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.500
for Newton shear stress law.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:58.640
You're going to convert this
summation into integration.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:00.950
You choose your own
coordinate, whether you

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:04.520
want to work with energy, you
want work with the momentum.

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:08.330
If you recall, for Fourier law,
we work with the frequency.

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:13.710
For Newton shear stress law, we
actually work with vx, vy, vz.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:19.950
And here, the best is still work
with the energy Ek in this case

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:23.070
because the f itself
depends on f0.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:24.990
f0 has the Ek inside.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:28.620
And that will be the
easiest way to deal with.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:32.700
So I'm not going to go
through the intermediate step.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:35.860
And I'm going to give
you the final result.

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:45.770
What I have is a coupled
term, L11 minus 1 over q,

00:10:45.770 --> 00:11:02.340
and dEf dx, plus dEc
dx, plus L12 must dt dx.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:08.350
So they current
flow is coupled to

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:11.580
electrochemical potential
gradient and temperature

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:13.190
gradient.

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:16.170
Ef plus Ec, again, is d mu.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:22.760
So here is du mu dx minus q.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:27.800
And sometimes it's also
written as the dq, mu over q.

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:31.610
Let's use a capital Phi dx.

00:11:31.610 --> 00:11:40.410
And say this is called the
EMF, Electromotive Force.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:45.720
It's combination of
electrostatic and chemical

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:49.350
potential gradient.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:54.300
So here, because remember,
these are the energy units.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:57.120
When I talk about
electrostatic potential,

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:01.480
I do actually Ec divide by
q, normalized by charge.

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:04.650
So there are different
units, and even

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:07.770
they all called
potential energy unit.

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:12.270
And the other is energy
divided by coulomb.

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:18.120
So I have those coefficient the
coupled transport, L11, L12--

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:26.010
let me give you, say,
expression for L11, L12.

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:30.720
And L11, after you do all
your math integration,

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:40.140
you say it's 1/3 q squared, 0
to infinite, and v squared tau,

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:49.860
and dEk and df0 dEk and dEk.

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:57.810
And if I have a parabolic
band, remember the energy Ek

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:03.830
is 1/2 mv squared over 2.

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:07.820
Or if I do, say,
in quantum term,

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:14.690
it's, say, 2m h bar squared k
because mv is the momentum h

00:13:14.690 --> 00:13:15.860
bar k.

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:24.160
So you can convert this v into
Ek and do your integration.

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:26.800
So this is one coefficient.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:37.240
And the other coefficient,
L12, is minus q divide by 3T,

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:54.400
0 to infinite, v squared tau,
and Ek minus Ef, df0 dEk dEk.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:58.840
So if I know the
density of states--

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:02.230
oh, I should have
density of states--

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:04.930
density of states, dEk.

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:12.880
So if you know the
density of states,

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:16.870
you know relaxation time, you
can calculate those transport

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:19.650
coefficient.

00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:25.800
And if you recall, when we
talk about the electron energy

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:28.770
levels, Kronig-Penney
model, those

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:31.080
are the different
ways to calculate

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.000
the details of
the band structure

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:34.665
of different materials.

00:14:34.665 --> 00:14:38.550
And from that, you can
get density of states.

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:42.720
And from density of states,
you dope the material.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:46.710
So based on doping, you can
determine where is your Ef.

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:51.550
Ef can be adjusted by doping.

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:54.970
And this is a harder to
calculate tau, relaxation time.

00:14:54.970 --> 00:15:00.640
We discussed, in fact, say,
there are really not good way--

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:06.220
not many calculation of the
relaxation time for electrons.

00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:10.600
And because, let's say, once you
go to the scattering process,

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:14.680
it's always a higher order and
more difficult to deal with.

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:17.320
But say in general the
framework is there,

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:19.210
like you do density
function theory.

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:21.640
You can calculate
the band structure

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:25.270
of different materials and
calculate density of states,

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:27.700
Ef, all these other quantities.

00:15:27.700 --> 00:15:31.300
And from there, you can
potentially also calculate

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:35.050
the scattering rate using
the perturbation method,

00:15:35.050 --> 00:15:38.280
like Fermi golden rule.

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:43.230
So those are the
functional form we have.

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:48.390
And let's look a little
more into details

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:53.415
what the solution tells us.

00:15:56.400 --> 00:16:00.210
So first, let's look at
a constant temperature,

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:06.260
T. There's no
temperature gradient.

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:10.040
And in the case of
constant temperature,

00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:12.230
let's drop the first term--

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:14.570
the second term here.

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:19.220
Then the current flux is
related to electrochemical

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:21.740
potential gradient.

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:25.535
I further will distinguish
it, say, metal.

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:39.020
And in metal, the electron
density is very high.

00:16:39.020 --> 00:16:45.240
So effectively there is no
change in the electron density.

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:49.410
And if you recall,
the difference

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:52.860
say between metal
and semiconductor,

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:55.650
when we discussed before,
this is the electron energy

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:56.610
momentum.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:17:03.120
Let's say here is
the, say, electron

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:06.960
band structure, the energy
[INAUDIBLE] the relation.

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:13.839
In case of metal, my Fermi
level is deep into the band.

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:14.829
This is my Ef.

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:23.430
And most of this charge
below the Fermi level,

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:25.950
they don't have, because
they are more mobile.

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:27.869
They are failed states.

00:17:27.869 --> 00:17:30.570
So only about the kt
near the Fermi level

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:34.280
are the charge that
actually moves.

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:41.280
So in this case, if I
look at the term Ef,

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:43.220
which is the chemical potential.

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:47.720
The number density of
the charge in the metal

00:17:47.720 --> 00:17:49.200
doesn't change much.

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:51.620
So dEf dx can be dropped out.

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:56.390
It's really electrostatic
potential pretty much that

00:17:56.390 --> 00:18:00.290
is driving the charge flow.

00:18:00.290 --> 00:18:06.470
And if I look at the transport,
essentially this is Ohm's law.

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:09.770
Jk or the current
flux is proportional

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:14.240
to the electrochemical
potential gradient.

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:18.830
And L11 is the
electric conductivity.

00:18:18.830 --> 00:18:24.920
So I have L11 is the
electric conductivity.

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:30.590
And in the case of metal,
this differentiation

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:35.120
is non-zero near
the Fermi level.

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:38.660
And other places, they
are all pretty much 0.

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:47.810
So I can approximate that
this integral with 1/3 tau f.

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:51.950
This is the relaxation
time at the Fermi level,

00:18:51.950 --> 00:18:57.590
and density of states at the
Fermi level, E squared vf,

00:18:57.590 --> 00:19:01.600
velocity at the Fermi level.

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:04.200
So this is the
electric conductivity.

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:06.720
And in which you
can further written

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:10.260
because let's say you can
relate density of states

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:14.440
to the electron number
density in the material.

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:19.260
So this is nE
squared, say, m tau f.

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:28.840
So the electric
conductivity in metal

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:33.070
is mainly the carrier
density doesn't change much.

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:36.310
It's the electrostatic
potential that's

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:38.800
driving the current flow.

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:40.960
Now if we look at
the semiconductor.

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:57.850
And in semiconductor, I have
both the carrier density

00:19:57.850 --> 00:20:02.560
and the electrostatic
potential gradient.

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:05.500
They all could vary
in the semiconductor

00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:07.510
when you make a P-N conjunction.

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:10.450
For example, there
is a large change

00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:14.540
in the band edge of
the semiconductor.

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:19.060
So both term are changing.

00:20:19.060 --> 00:20:25.390
And the current density
is often written

00:20:25.390 --> 00:20:29.890
as rather than write
into just this term,

00:20:29.890 --> 00:20:34.030
you're actually
sometimes split this out.

00:20:34.030 --> 00:20:37.180
One is related to
density, E of dx.

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:39.320
That's related to
carrier density.

00:20:39.320 --> 00:20:43.130
This one is related to
electrostatic potential.

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:48.940
So in semiconductor, the
famous equation people

00:20:48.940 --> 00:20:55.000
saw is q and mu epsilon of x.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:21:01.120
That's the electric field, which
is the gradient of negative,

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:04.120
just say, 1 over q dEc dx.

00:21:08.860 --> 00:21:18.186
And plus a diffusion
term, dn dx, because dEf--

00:21:18.186 --> 00:21:20.740
Ef determine the
carrier density.

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:23.710
There is a fixed relationship
between Ef and n.

00:21:23.710 --> 00:21:31.090
So dEf dx is dn dx, and with
the diffusion coefficient a.

00:21:31.090 --> 00:21:33.790
So here is diffusivity.

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:47.030
And here is the reason
it's written in metal,

00:21:47.030 --> 00:21:49.310
we don't talk about the mu--

00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:51.590
we talk about sigma.

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:56.140
In semiconductor,
this N changes a lot.

00:21:56.140 --> 00:21:58.280
So you normalize the sigma.

00:21:58.280 --> 00:22:04.240
This q and mu gives you
sigma, electric conductivity.

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:07.000
So here is sigma.

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:09.910
And this mu is the mobility.

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:22.710
So sigma equals qn mu.

00:22:22.710 --> 00:22:27.993
And if you go back to here,
because sigma is ne squared m,

00:22:27.993 --> 00:22:29.070
tau f.

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:38.160
So in general, mu is
related to m, n tau--

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:40.920
so no, no n--

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:41.700
e tau.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:54.820
Say, E tau divided by m.

00:22:54.820 --> 00:22:59.290
So measured mobility,
effectively you

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:02.320
say in this you have
the effective mass,

00:23:02.320 --> 00:23:04.240
you have the relaxation time.

00:23:04.240 --> 00:23:08.800
So it's a measure of
the relaxation time

00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:12.175
and it will give you information
about the effective mass.

00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:23.080
And now you can say, in
fact, both of those terms

00:23:23.080 --> 00:23:24.460
have the same coefficient.

00:23:24.460 --> 00:23:26.720
I'm just splitting them up.

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:30.070
So this is a diffusivity
and mobility are

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:32.240
related to each other.

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:36.330
And the relation between
diffusivity and mobility,

00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:42.940
a, is kb t over e times mu.

00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:45.025
And this is called
the Einstein relation.

00:23:54.640 --> 00:23:56.500
It's a general
relationship, let's say,

00:23:56.500 --> 00:24:02.070
between the mobility
and diffusivity.

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:03.630
Einstein didn't work on this.

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:10.400
What he really worked on
is the Brownian motion.

00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:13.000
And in the case of
Brownian motion,

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:16.270
they are particle diffusivity.

00:24:16.270 --> 00:24:21.790
And think about the
milk molecules in water.

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:29.775
The milk molecule diffusivity is
related to viscosity of fluids.

00:24:29.775 --> 00:24:31.480
So this is a general--

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:34.960
in fact, I say the
generalization of this,

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:38.600
as I said before, Einstein
started this other direction--

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:40.270
linear response theory.

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:45.790
And this is a part
of, say, dissipation--

00:24:45.790 --> 00:24:48.220
fluctuation dissipation theory.

00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:49.910
General, very general.

00:24:49.910 --> 00:24:51.700
So this is a--

00:24:51.700 --> 00:24:56.020
diffusion is a
fluctuation process.

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:59.630
Mobility is a dissipation
coalition process.

00:24:59.630 --> 00:25:02.050
So that's a fluctuation
dissipation theorem.

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:05.860
That's one example of
fluctuation dissipation

00:25:05.860 --> 00:25:06.910
theorem.

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:10.300
And Einstein relation
between the mass diffusion

00:25:10.300 --> 00:25:12.898
and the viscosity
is another example.

00:25:12.898 --> 00:25:15.190
So there are many examples
of fluctuation dissipation--

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:16.760
very general.

00:25:16.760 --> 00:25:21.630
So what the electrical engineer
do is solving this equation.

00:25:21.630 --> 00:25:25.060
Here is called drift diffusion.

00:25:25.060 --> 00:25:27.420
So this is the drift
the diffusion equation.

00:25:38.190 --> 00:25:43.650
OK, so if you look at a
lot of, say-- in fact,

00:25:43.650 --> 00:25:47.520
even nowadays, they see MOS.

00:25:51.030 --> 00:25:57.390
MOS-- you all know, it's a metal
oxide field effect transistors.

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:01.500
And even with the gate
now shrank to tens--

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:03.270
I don't know exactly
how many tens--

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:07.380
20 or 30 nanometers,
the industry

00:26:07.380 --> 00:26:08.685
is still using this equation.

00:26:11.420 --> 00:26:14.210
And they just tweak
the parameters.

00:26:14.210 --> 00:26:16.640
They tweak the mobility
parameter, diffuse field

00:26:16.640 --> 00:26:21.710
parameters just to fit the
device characteristics.

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:24.080
And they know it's
not [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:24.080 --> 00:26:28.070
because the electron mean
free path could be larger.

00:26:28.070 --> 00:26:31.400
But it's building
in many softwares.

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:34.310
It's amazing that they can still
use it to design the circuits.

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:39.905
So that's the semiconductor
drift diffusion equation.

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:47.180
If you design solar sail,
this will be the equation

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:48.800
you're going to solve--

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:49.715
most of solar cells.

00:26:52.220 --> 00:26:57.500
And so now let's look at the--
the first case we discuss

00:26:57.500 --> 00:26:59.870
is the constant temperature.

00:26:59.870 --> 00:27:09.200
And let's discuss when the
temperature is not constant,

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:13.460
it's a function of x.

00:27:13.460 --> 00:27:18.440
So in this case,
I have a material.

00:27:18.440 --> 00:27:21.650
I have a temperature gradient.

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:27.470
And in the x direction,
I have heat conduction.

00:27:27.470 --> 00:27:38.390
And what happens-- first, if
I open circuit, Je equals 0.

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:43.890
So what do you have if
you have an open circuit

00:27:43.890 --> 00:27:46.560
with J-- or not e-- q.

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:49.230
e is for electron.

00:27:49.230 --> 00:27:51.270
q is a more general.

00:27:51.270 --> 00:27:56.490
Sometimes I switch because
there are not enough symbols.

00:27:56.490 --> 00:27:59.490
And when you deal with
heat, you like to use a q.

00:27:59.490 --> 00:28:02.610
And you deal with the
charge, you like to use e,

00:28:02.610 --> 00:28:06.600
but the e is negative,
so all this complication.

00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:13.410
But open circuit-- if you
look at my flux equation here

00:28:13.410 --> 00:28:16.980
and what I have is-- this is 0.

00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:20.400
But I have two terms
on the right-hand side.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:21.870
dt, dx is not 0.

00:28:21.870 --> 00:28:24.210
So I will have d phi, dx--

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:27.340
non-zero-- that balance it.

00:28:27.340 --> 00:28:30.310
So what I really
have, in this case,

00:28:30.310 --> 00:28:34.860
I have my Seebeck
coefficient is defined

00:28:34.860 --> 00:28:40.110
as the negative d phi, dx.

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:45.780
This is, essentially--
sometimes we use, say,

00:28:45.780 --> 00:28:48.570
electric field-- the epsilon x.

00:28:48.570 --> 00:28:52.740
But, say, I use epsilon
x before so I'm just

00:28:52.740 --> 00:28:55.618
going d phi, dx and dt, dx.

00:29:00.560 --> 00:29:04.550
And this one is the
Seebeck coefficient.

00:29:04.550 --> 00:29:09.020
And it equals L12, L11.

00:29:09.020 --> 00:29:11.580
And remember, L1-- my sigma.

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:15.320
So if I look at this, I
substitute the expression

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:18.740
for those coefficients.

00:29:18.740 --> 00:29:22.130
If you say the difference
of the two-- v squared tau,

00:29:22.130 --> 00:29:26.240
v squared tau, d
and d, df0, dEk.

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:29.430
The difference is
just Ek minus Ef.

00:29:29.430 --> 00:29:33.690
And of course, in front,
there are small differences.

00:29:33.690 --> 00:29:41.070
So what I have here is
I will have e over tau--

00:29:41.070 --> 00:29:42.705
eT-- T is temperature.

00:29:42.705 --> 00:29:46.390
E is the positive charge.

00:29:46.390 --> 00:29:52.380
No sign problem-- v squared
tau and the Ek minus Ef.

00:29:55.260 --> 00:30:03.540
df0, dEk, and density
of states Ek, dEk.

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:09.270
I write it because I want
to explain what it means.

00:30:09.270 --> 00:30:18.100
v squared tau,
df0, dEk, dEk, dEk.

00:30:22.140 --> 00:30:26.670
So you can say that this
is the Seebeck effect--

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:34.440
Seebeck coefficient.

00:30:40.860 --> 00:30:45.270
And back was discovered in 1821.

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:48.825
That was about same time
electricity was studied.

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:55.200
So basically, when you
apply a temperature gradient

00:30:55.200 --> 00:31:01.240
to material, you generate
electrochemical potential

00:31:01.240 --> 00:31:01.740
gradient.

00:31:05.040 --> 00:31:06.870
And what happens
now, if you look

00:31:06.870 --> 00:31:12.780
at this is a temperature
gradient driver diffusion--

00:31:12.780 --> 00:31:13.860
current.

00:31:13.860 --> 00:31:18.210
So when I have
higher temperature,

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:21.030
the electrons diffuse faster.

00:31:21.030 --> 00:31:22.590
They come to this side.

00:31:22.590 --> 00:31:24.510
There are more on this side.

00:31:24.510 --> 00:31:28.300
When there are more, the
chemical potential will change,

00:31:28.300 --> 00:31:30.330
the electrostatic
potential will change.

00:31:30.330 --> 00:31:34.720
They are readjusted to
resist the diffusion.

00:31:34.720 --> 00:31:36.990
So this term is
resist diffusion.

00:31:36.990 --> 00:31:40.570
This is diffusion--
driving force.

00:31:40.570 --> 00:31:44.520
So at the end, you have
a left open circuit.

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:47.920
Left charge equals 0.

00:31:47.920 --> 00:31:49.750
The current equals 0.

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:53.980
And you have voltage
develop across the material.

00:31:56.670 --> 00:31:59.970
And so that's what a
phenomenological explanation.

00:31:59.970 --> 00:32:03.540
Now if I connect this
to the band structure,

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:06.490
here is my conduction band.

00:32:06.490 --> 00:32:09.330
And I just look at
the conduction band.

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:13.320
Here is where the
chemical potential level--

00:32:13.320 --> 00:32:15.720
Fermi level-- is.

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:18.000
And Ek minus Ef--

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:19.800
so it's this difference.

00:32:19.800 --> 00:32:22.440
I said this is--

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:23.130
Ef itself.

00:32:26.200 --> 00:32:28.090
Ek is here.

00:32:28.090 --> 00:32:32.000
So I have Ek minus Ef.

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:34.070
So is it an average energy.

00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:37.680
You can say here,
this is Ek minus Ef.

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:40.110
So the rest is just
the-- here, you

00:32:40.110 --> 00:32:44.000
can think about that each
electron at certain energy

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:48.740
level has a specific energy
dependent spectral contribution

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:50.750
to electric conductivity.

00:32:50.750 --> 00:32:55.070
And I'm averaging that to
the differentiable electric

00:32:55.070 --> 00:32:58.210
conductivity there.

00:32:58.210 --> 00:33:01.810
So Seebeck coefficient
is the average

00:33:01.810 --> 00:33:07.320
of the electron energy relative
to the chemical potential.

00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:14.610
And this, also-- the
electron energy to chemical

00:33:14.610 --> 00:33:17.850
potential is the heat.

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:23.200
So it's the average
energy per charge.

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:25.810
And you use thermocouple.

00:33:25.810 --> 00:33:29.770
So next time, you can say you're
measuring a voltage rating out

00:33:29.770 --> 00:33:32.290
of thermocouple,
fundamentally, is

00:33:32.290 --> 00:33:38.050
average-- is actually the heat
per charge carried you measure.

00:33:38.050 --> 00:33:39.850
And heat per charge
carried is also

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:42.490
the entropy per charge carried.

00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:47.185
So the Seebeck coefficient is
really entropy of the charge.

00:33:51.410 --> 00:33:56.300
And with that, you can
also appreciate why metal

00:33:56.300 --> 00:33:58.880
has a low Seebeck coefficient.

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:00.880
So if I look at, say, metal--

00:34:04.150 --> 00:34:10.120
I draw the Fermi
level of metal here.

00:34:10.120 --> 00:34:17.480
And if the electron is
above the Fermi level,

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:20.040
they have positive heat.

00:34:20.040 --> 00:34:24.090
If electron below the Fermi
level, they have negative heat.

00:34:24.090 --> 00:34:28.320
But in metal, you are averaging
over all positive, negative.

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:29.489
They cancel each other.

00:34:32.590 --> 00:34:37.460
So that's why metal has a
low Seebeck coefficient.

00:34:37.460 --> 00:34:41.810
And in a semiconductor,
my hole is here.

00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:49.400
My Ef is very close to the
conduction band or very close

00:34:49.400 --> 00:34:50.790
to the valence band.

00:34:50.790 --> 00:34:53.989
So normally, I want the
one type of carrier,

00:34:53.989 --> 00:34:57.970
and they do not
cancel each other.

00:34:57.970 --> 00:35:02.250
But in fact, in most
thermoelectric effect,

00:35:02.250 --> 00:35:05.220
you actually don't put
very heavily into the band.

00:35:08.310 --> 00:35:10.380
So your chemical wouldn't
get into the very

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:13.710
close to the bottom
conduction band, but above it.

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:16.980
And in that case, those
carriers in the bottom--

00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:20.280
below that camera potential--
is actually harmful.

00:35:20.280 --> 00:35:22.650
They do cancel.

00:35:22.650 --> 00:35:27.120
So that's why in some of
those structure, people want--

00:35:27.120 --> 00:35:30.000
rather than this kind of
parabolic [INAUDIBLE],,

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:33.000
but if you have those kind
of densities [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:34.950
sharp features,
you will be better.

00:35:38.080 --> 00:35:39.910
That's the quantum size effect.

00:35:39.910 --> 00:35:42.460
And we can go--

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:46.360
discuss more size effects
in the next chapter.

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:50.400
So this is the Seebeck effect.

00:35:50.400 --> 00:35:56.070
And that's the associated
with the charge flow.

00:35:56.070 --> 00:36:01.080
I have, also, the
heat carried by--

00:36:01.080 --> 00:36:04.710
due to the drive
the charge flow.

00:36:04.710 --> 00:36:08.070
And now, let's look at the
other, which is the heat

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:10.590
carried--

00:36:10.590 --> 00:36:11.190
heat flow.

00:36:18.760 --> 00:36:21.250
And what is the heat flow?

00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:28.180
I already mentioned that the Ek
minus Ef is the heat carried.

00:36:28.180 --> 00:36:35.650
And in fact, you could write
down the energy equals--

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:37.790
the first law
tells us the energy

00:36:37.790 --> 00:36:43.495
equals the heat plus Efdn--

00:36:48.890 --> 00:36:52.715
the chemical potential
times the change

00:36:52.715 --> 00:36:54.950
in the number of particles.

00:36:54.950 --> 00:36:56.450
That's the first law.

00:36:56.450 --> 00:36:58.970
You can also add electrostatic.

00:36:58.970 --> 00:37:02.840
So basically, the
energy in the material

00:37:02.840 --> 00:37:06.770
is, say, n times the
energy per charge.

00:37:06.770 --> 00:37:09.140
That's my E here.

00:37:09.140 --> 00:37:14.900
So you can say, dQ per
charge is E minus Ef.

00:37:14.900 --> 00:37:17.900
So it's really--
the heat is here.

00:37:17.900 --> 00:37:21.260
Ek minus Ef is my heat.

00:37:24.510 --> 00:37:30.770
So it's heat carried
per, say, quantum state.

00:37:30.770 --> 00:37:34.460
So with that, I can
go to calculate,

00:37:34.460 --> 00:37:39.040
what's the total amount of
heat when the current flows

00:37:39.040 --> 00:37:40.330
in the material?

00:37:40.330 --> 00:37:45.700
So JQ-- I think my book
uses the one is the Je.

00:37:45.700 --> 00:37:47.320
The other is a small q.

00:37:47.320 --> 00:37:51.530
But since I've been
keeping Jq as for charge,

00:37:51.530 --> 00:37:55.450
so now I'm doing the capital Q.

00:37:55.450 --> 00:38:05.920
So JQ, then, is 2
over v, kx, ky, kz.

00:38:05.920 --> 00:38:10.750
And if you look at it here, when
we do the charge for quantum

00:38:10.750 --> 00:38:17.740
state it's Q. And
here what I have is

00:38:17.740 --> 00:38:21.880
the heat is Ek minus Ef and Vxf.

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:31.270
So again, I'm applying
my general expression,

00:38:31.270 --> 00:38:33.190
general way how to
find out, what's

00:38:33.190 --> 00:38:37.430
the flux for
different quantities?

00:38:37.430 --> 00:38:41.260
And you go through the
conversion of the summation

00:38:41.260 --> 00:38:44.830
into integration again,
and what you find out

00:38:44.830 --> 00:38:50.470
is I have L21 minus d phi--

00:38:50.470 --> 00:38:55.396
again, d phi, dx because
I already have f--

00:38:55.396 --> 00:39:01.150
and plus L22 minus dt, dx.

00:39:04.330 --> 00:39:06.650
So this is the current flux.

00:39:06.650 --> 00:39:10.090
So I have a set
coupled expression--

00:39:10.090 --> 00:39:18.220
J for the electrical is L11 d
phi, dx and then, 12 dt, dx.

00:39:18.220 --> 00:39:23.060
And for the heat flow,
have 21, L22, dt, dx.

00:39:25.650 --> 00:39:28.770
And in fact, that there is
a very fundamental relation

00:39:28.770 --> 00:39:30.600
between those coefficients.

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:34.800
Any time you see
coupled expressions--

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:37.860
say the charge is
coupled with temperature.

00:39:37.860 --> 00:39:40.440
That's a cross term.

00:39:40.440 --> 00:39:45.670
If you write your cross term
into a general entropy--

00:39:45.670 --> 00:39:49.050
so this is a generalized
thermodynamics--

00:39:49.050 --> 00:39:54.390
the general thermodynamic, the
driving force for the flux--

00:39:54.390 --> 00:39:58.920
the heat flux and any
other flux, charge flux--

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:02.460
is the entropy gradient there.

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:08.310
And for entropy, if you
recall, it's d, dx, 1 over T

00:40:08.310 --> 00:40:10.170
and the q, right?

00:40:10.170 --> 00:40:14.260
So it's T in the denominator.

00:40:14.260 --> 00:40:19.950
So any of this-- if you write
into generalized entropy

00:40:19.950 --> 00:40:23.550
as a driving force, then
this cross coefficients

00:40:23.550 --> 00:40:25.830
are always equal.

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:30.460
And this is the fundamental,
say, Onsager relation.

00:40:30.460 --> 00:40:36.900
So here, we have
Onsager relation.

00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:45.340
And in this case, because
I'm not writing, say, L21--

00:40:45.340 --> 00:40:47.970
say, dt, dx in terms of entropy.

00:40:47.970 --> 00:40:50.700
If I use the entropy,
say they should equal.

00:40:50.700 --> 00:40:53.890
But because I'm not
using the entropy,

00:40:53.890 --> 00:40:58.560
so I have TL21
equals T times L12.

00:41:02.210 --> 00:41:06.950
So any time-- this, like I
said, any couple the process,

00:41:06.950 --> 00:41:10.340
you can always ask, where
is your Onsager relation?

00:41:10.340 --> 00:41:16.190
Next time you go to seminar, a
guy talk about piezoelectric.

00:41:16.190 --> 00:41:19.820
Piezoelectric is the
electrical and the pressure.

00:41:19.820 --> 00:41:23.210
There got to be that coupling
coefficient from electrical,

00:41:23.210 --> 00:41:26.330
to pressure, to force all
from force to electrical.

00:41:26.330 --> 00:41:29.690
Their coefficients always
obey the Onsager relation.

00:41:34.390 --> 00:41:37.630
And so that's the form.

00:41:37.630 --> 00:41:40.160
And I'm not even going to
write down this expression.

00:41:40.160 --> 00:41:41.950
It's all in the book.

00:41:41.950 --> 00:41:46.270
And again, let's look
at the implication.

00:41:52.060 --> 00:42:01.060
And if I first look at a
constant temperature case,

00:42:01.060 --> 00:42:03.520
again, I flow current
through the material.

00:42:06.160 --> 00:42:09.650
And this term drops out.

00:42:09.650 --> 00:42:11.870
This term drops out.

00:42:11.870 --> 00:42:17.315
But I can replace
d phi, dx by Jq,

00:42:17.315 --> 00:42:22.970
then I have a relation
between the heat current

00:42:22.970 --> 00:42:26.720
and the electric current.

00:42:26.720 --> 00:42:30.850
So if you do that relation--

00:42:30.850 --> 00:42:34.870
say you have, here,
you have the heat

00:42:34.870 --> 00:42:41.950
current will be related to
the electric current L21, L11,

00:42:41.950 --> 00:42:44.090
and Je--

00:42:44.090 --> 00:42:44.590
Jq.

00:42:48.650 --> 00:42:51.610
So this is the Peltier effect.

00:42:51.610 --> 00:42:53.400
So when you fly--

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:58.290
flow a current through
a conductor associated

00:42:58.290 --> 00:43:03.810
with current, there is
heat carried by the charge.

00:43:03.810 --> 00:43:07.720
And this is reversible when you
change the current direction.

00:43:07.720 --> 00:43:09.960
So this is the
Peltier coefficient--

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:15.930
coefficient.

00:43:22.790 --> 00:43:25.660
And this is the-- the
effect itself is--

00:43:25.660 --> 00:43:28.830
the effect itself actually
don't manifest just

00:43:28.830 --> 00:43:31.150
in the same material.

00:43:31.150 --> 00:43:32.590
And what happens
is that when you

00:43:32.590 --> 00:43:34.150
put the two material together--

00:43:34.150 --> 00:43:36.550
1 and material 2--

00:43:36.550 --> 00:43:40.060
and you pass a current,
now, at the junction,

00:43:40.060 --> 00:43:44.600
you do your energy balance
because the Peltier difference.

00:43:44.600 --> 00:43:47.260
So there is more heat
flow out or more heat flow

00:43:47.260 --> 00:43:50.990
in at the junction because
of this difference.

00:43:50.990 --> 00:43:52.810
So you have cooling
or heating depend

00:43:52.810 --> 00:43:55.390
on the current direction,
which is reversible as you

00:43:55.390 --> 00:43:57.060
change your current direction.

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:02.390
And of course, if
you look at this,

00:44:02.390 --> 00:44:08.480
because our Seebeck effect
is L12, L11 and L21 is TL12.

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:11.030
So I know this pi--

00:44:11.030 --> 00:44:13.520
the Peltier
coefficient is actually

00:44:13.520 --> 00:44:17.850
related to Seebeck just times
the absolute temperature.

00:44:17.850 --> 00:44:22.420
So these two are
related to each other.

00:44:22.420 --> 00:44:25.665
And this was-- say, this is
called the Kelvin relation.

00:44:31.930 --> 00:44:36.370
But it's one, special example
of the general Onsager

00:44:36.370 --> 00:44:39.370
relation I just said.

00:44:39.370 --> 00:44:42.640
Kelvin is much
earlier than Onsager.

00:44:42.640 --> 00:44:46.675
Onsager [INAUDIBLE] also for
the Onsager relation he develop.

00:44:51.180 --> 00:44:56.510
So we have the example of the--

00:44:56.510 --> 00:44:59.400
the Kelvin relation is an
example of the Onsager.

00:44:59.400 --> 00:45:01.800
And I think I
mentioned this before.

00:45:01.800 --> 00:45:05.430
Kelvin is, say, is Lord Kelvin.

00:45:05.430 --> 00:45:07.695
And it's the same as Thompson.

00:45:12.170 --> 00:45:15.420
If you check, he wrote over
500 papers in his lifetime

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:19.580
in the 1800s.

00:45:19.580 --> 00:45:21.950
It's not easy even to type it.

00:45:27.890 --> 00:45:32.930
OK, so that's the one case.

00:45:32.930 --> 00:45:41.720
And second case, if I just
combine these two equation,

00:45:41.720 --> 00:45:47.980
I can always eliminate the
electrochemical potential-- d

00:45:47.980 --> 00:45:49.390
phi, dx.

00:45:49.390 --> 00:45:54.870
d phi, dx, in turn, express Qp
flux in terms of current flux.

00:45:54.870 --> 00:45:59.450
So what I have, if I
eliminate the d phi, dx,

00:45:59.450 --> 00:46:01.430
what I have in general--

00:46:01.430 --> 00:46:07.640
JQ equals pi Jq.

00:46:07.640 --> 00:46:09.290
So that's the Peltier.

00:46:09.290 --> 00:46:15.530
And then I have a the
next term is L11, L12--

00:46:15.530 --> 00:46:27.486
L22 minus L12, L21 divided
by L11 and times dt,

00:46:27.486 --> 00:46:34.810
dx here in the front-- dt, dx.

00:46:34.810 --> 00:46:37.540
So what it means--

00:46:37.540 --> 00:46:39.400
normally, you
would probably tend

00:46:39.400 --> 00:46:44.800
to think L22 is your
thermal conductivity.

00:46:44.800 --> 00:46:48.990
That's not the case
because you can say,

00:46:48.990 --> 00:46:52.110
when I flew a current
through the material,

00:46:52.110 --> 00:46:56.600
the heat flow is-- one term
is the Peltier heat flow,

00:46:56.600 --> 00:47:00.300
and second term is a
temperature gradient-driven.

00:47:00.300 --> 00:47:02.100
And so this temperature
gradient-driven

00:47:02.100 --> 00:47:04.290
is not just L22.

00:47:04.290 --> 00:47:07.000
It's this all combined together.

00:47:07.000 --> 00:47:12.810
And so here, this term is the Ke
is electronic because here, I'm

00:47:12.810 --> 00:47:14.820
modeling electrons.

00:47:14.820 --> 00:47:18.080
The phonons, we
already did before.

00:47:18.080 --> 00:47:21.240
Phonon, we did the thermal
conductivity expression.

00:47:21.240 --> 00:47:26.210
Electronic thermal conductivity
is the Ke is this blob there.

00:47:26.210 --> 00:47:28.550
It's a compilation of
three coefficients.

00:47:34.200 --> 00:47:44.280
So if you-- in metal and
you can show that I say,

00:47:44.280 --> 00:47:47.520
OK, Ke, again, say
all this roughly

00:47:47.520 --> 00:47:51.330
give you 1/3cv squared tau.

00:47:51.330 --> 00:47:54.430
And tau is the
energy reaction time.

00:47:54.430 --> 00:48:00.660
And you can look at all those
and actually find the relation

00:48:00.660 --> 00:48:03.510
because eventually,
what's connecting them

00:48:03.510 --> 00:48:07.380
if you look at their
solution, f has a tau.

00:48:07.380 --> 00:48:10.140
f itself has a tau, has a v.

00:48:10.140 --> 00:48:12.420
At the end, you have
[INAUDIBLE] states.

00:48:12.420 --> 00:48:15.810
So the difference between those
different coefficients-- just

00:48:15.810 --> 00:48:18.730
whether it's
averaging the charge,

00:48:18.730 --> 00:48:21.270
it's averaging in the energy.

00:48:21.270 --> 00:48:28.430
So because of that, this Ke
and the thermal conductivity

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:32.360
and the electrical conductivity
are actually related.

00:48:32.360 --> 00:48:34.350
If you recall in
the last lecture,

00:48:34.350 --> 00:48:38.750
we mentioned for the
gas, it's the same.

00:48:38.750 --> 00:48:43.580
The viscosity and thermal
conductivity are related.

00:48:43.580 --> 00:48:45.650
So all of this is
because you come

00:48:45.650 --> 00:48:48.200
from pretty much the same
equation, except that I'm

00:48:48.200 --> 00:48:50.630
doing different averaging.

00:48:50.630 --> 00:48:56.870
So what I have in
this is Ke sigma T.

00:48:56.870 --> 00:49:01.310
And in particular, in metal,
this one is a constant,

00:49:01.310 --> 00:49:09.590
and this is pi squared over
1/3 and KB over E squared.

00:49:09.590 --> 00:49:14.990
And there is 2.45 10
to the minus eight

00:49:14.990 --> 00:49:20.090
O watt Kelvin squared.

00:49:20.090 --> 00:49:21.890
And this is the Lorenz number--

00:49:27.545 --> 00:49:28.045
Lorenz.

00:49:34.150 --> 00:49:37.540
And this is called the
Wiedeman-Franz law.

00:49:45.020 --> 00:49:45.520
Wiedeman.

00:49:53.220 --> 00:49:56.490
So in metal, the
electrical conductivity

00:49:56.490 --> 00:49:58.500
divided by electronic
contribution

00:49:58.500 --> 00:50:02.480
to thermal conductivity divided
by electric conductivity

00:50:02.480 --> 00:50:04.950
divide normalize temperature
because specific heat

00:50:04.950 --> 00:50:08.230
of electron is linear
proportional to temperature.

00:50:08.230 --> 00:50:12.230
And that ratio gives
you a constant.

00:50:12.230 --> 00:50:15.410
And if you check a lot of
material metal, in metal,

00:50:15.410 --> 00:50:18.680
the dominant heat
carrier is electronics.

00:50:18.680 --> 00:50:23.380
And phonons contribute probably
less than 10%, in general.

00:50:23.380 --> 00:50:26.980
And you see that, indeed,
the electrical-- and this

00:50:26.980 --> 00:50:30.620
is very close to this ratio.

00:50:30.620 --> 00:50:34.490
So a simple, easy
way if you don't know

00:50:34.490 --> 00:50:38.150
the thermoconductivity,
which is harder to measure,

00:50:38.150 --> 00:50:40.190
to measure electrical
conductivity.

00:50:40.190 --> 00:50:41.600
It can give you an estimation.

00:50:44.180 --> 00:50:48.110
And of course, say, if
you do semiconductor,

00:50:48.110 --> 00:50:49.920
that's a little bit different.

00:50:49.920 --> 00:50:55.820
And if you do semiconductor,
you have to go back

00:50:55.820 --> 00:50:58.100
to all this expression.

00:50:58.100 --> 00:51:01.280
And turns out that this
Lorenz number depends

00:51:01.280 --> 00:51:04.250
on carrier concentration.

00:51:04.250 --> 00:51:08.390
Because your Ef is
changing, different doping

00:51:08.390 --> 00:51:11.350
will change the Fermi level.

00:51:11.350 --> 00:51:13.810
And hence, say, all
those coefficient

00:51:13.810 --> 00:51:16.580
depends on Fermi level.

00:51:16.580 --> 00:51:18.560
But the range so far--

00:51:18.560 --> 00:51:21.320
this range so far
has not changed much.

00:51:21.320 --> 00:51:23.330
And the theory I've
seen is probably

00:51:23.330 --> 00:51:26.790
within factor of two difference.

00:51:26.790 --> 00:51:29.090
But, say, it's a
[INAUDIBLE],, but it's

00:51:29.090 --> 00:51:32.970
a theoretical possibility
that I have is

00:51:32.970 --> 00:51:36.510
how one can design
a material so that

00:51:36.510 --> 00:51:42.130
the electronic
thermoconductivity is 0,

00:51:42.130 --> 00:51:44.140
but the electric
conductivity is not 0.

00:51:47.530 --> 00:51:51.550
If you go to check
when this is 0,

00:51:51.550 --> 00:51:55.930
but you don't want the
L11 0, you actually say,

00:51:55.930 --> 00:51:57.205
functionally, it's possible.

00:52:00.100 --> 00:52:03.700
So this is how you actually
achieve this in real materials

00:52:03.700 --> 00:52:06.220
because that's very
important when you

00:52:06.220 --> 00:52:10.060
deal with thermal electrics.

00:52:10.060 --> 00:52:16.180
OK, so we have started from
the distribution function--

00:52:16.180 --> 00:52:17.710
the division.

00:52:17.710 --> 00:52:20.590
And we say, OK, if you write
all those coefficients,

00:52:20.590 --> 00:52:24.250
you should be able to
show the fundamental--

00:52:24.250 --> 00:52:30.340
this is, say, the coupled charge
to electrical and temperature

00:52:30.340 --> 00:52:33.280
gradient and the heat
carried by the charge

00:52:33.280 --> 00:52:37.300
to the electrochemical gradient
and temperature gradient.

00:52:37.300 --> 00:52:40.990
This is the Onsager
type of relations.

00:52:40.990 --> 00:52:44.330
And in many, many, say--

00:52:44.330 --> 00:52:47.320
in fact, that there are
many very interesting

00:52:47.320 --> 00:52:49.160
coupled process.

00:52:49.160 --> 00:52:52.570
And I mentioned that
piezoelectric recently

00:52:52.570 --> 00:52:55.660
say they are also
getting interested

00:52:55.660 --> 00:52:57.610
in this so-called spin.

00:52:57.610 --> 00:53:00.970
So electron spin
can also carry heat.

00:53:00.970 --> 00:53:03.850
And the spin Seebeck
there, that's

00:53:03.850 --> 00:53:05.560
become a so-called hot topic.

00:53:05.560 --> 00:53:08.110
If you're interested,
you can check literature.

00:53:08.110 --> 00:53:13.300
And magnetic, say,
we talk electron.

00:53:13.300 --> 00:53:14.530
We talk phonon.

00:53:14.530 --> 00:53:17.590
And there are also the
so-called magnons--

00:53:17.590 --> 00:53:23.170
the magnetic-- collecting
motion of the waves

00:53:23.170 --> 00:53:25.730
of the magnetic field.

00:53:25.730 --> 00:53:29.020
So all those, say,
effects could be coupled.

00:53:29.020 --> 00:53:32.800
And so this coupling probably
can lead some very interesting

00:53:32.800 --> 00:53:35.300
technology or effects.

00:53:35.300 --> 00:53:39.400
And so it's worth looking.

00:53:39.400 --> 00:53:42.520
And if you do heat
and mass transfer,

00:53:42.520 --> 00:53:44.770
again, those are coupled.

00:53:44.770 --> 00:53:47.140
And in the heat
and mass transfer,

00:53:47.140 --> 00:53:49.360
there is also
fundamental relation

00:53:49.360 --> 00:53:52.150
between the heat transfer
coefficient-- the coupling

00:53:52.150 --> 00:53:53.260
coefficient.

00:53:53.260 --> 00:53:56.290
That's, again,
example-- manifestation

00:53:56.290 --> 00:53:57.895
of the general Onsager relation.

00:54:00.540 --> 00:54:06.280
OK, so I want the dig in
and get in with more detail

00:54:06.280 --> 00:54:06.990
thermoelectric.

00:54:06.990 --> 00:54:14.070
I look at my progress so far, I
found we're one lecture behind.

00:54:14.070 --> 00:54:17.910
So what I'm doing is
trying to combine this

00:54:17.910 --> 00:54:19.680
into the
thermoelectrics lecture.

00:54:19.680 --> 00:54:22.590
I have 30 minutes.

00:54:22.590 --> 00:54:25.450
You already have the
thermoelectric effect,

00:54:25.450 --> 00:54:28.720
so it should be
not too difficult.

00:54:28.720 --> 00:54:32.280
And what I want to tell you
is, where does ZT come from?

00:54:49.110 --> 00:54:55.340
ZT-- so given what
you have learned,

00:54:55.340 --> 00:54:57.980
I can write the energy balance.

00:54:57.980 --> 00:55:00.890
And you can start
with differential

00:55:00.890 --> 00:55:03.950
because the equation
I have given you

00:55:03.950 --> 00:55:05.670
differentiable equations.

00:55:05.670 --> 00:55:07.820
So if I think about
it in general,

00:55:07.820 --> 00:55:16.100
when I have a conductor, I
take a differential element

00:55:16.100 --> 00:55:21.110
I have both heat and the
charge flow in the material.

00:55:21.110 --> 00:55:24.980
This is the x, x plus dx.

00:55:24.980 --> 00:55:31.430
Here I have the JQ
at the x, and here I

00:55:31.430 --> 00:55:34.160
have JQ at the x plus dx.

00:55:38.920 --> 00:55:41.860
And when I look at
the energy balance

00:55:41.860 --> 00:55:47.110
under an electric field,
I also have the change

00:55:47.110 --> 00:55:50.720
in electrochemical potential.

00:55:50.720 --> 00:55:53.970
So if I write my energy
balance for this problem,

00:55:53.970 --> 00:55:59.750
so I have JQ at the x
minus JQ at the x plus

00:55:59.750 --> 00:56:05.880
dx, that's the heat
carried by charge--

00:56:05.880 --> 00:56:09.040
flow in minus flow out.

00:56:09.040 --> 00:56:11.410
And I could have, also--

00:56:11.410 --> 00:56:13.780
of course, you can
think about you can have

00:56:13.780 --> 00:56:16.600
convection or that radiation.

00:56:16.600 --> 00:56:18.690
I'm neglecting that.

00:56:18.690 --> 00:56:22.800
But because I have electric
field, I cannot neglect that.

00:56:22.800 --> 00:56:26.130
So my field is the current--

00:56:26.130 --> 00:56:29.730
how much energy those
charge are losing

00:56:29.730 --> 00:56:36.540
is how much electrochemical
potential gradient

00:56:36.540 --> 00:56:38.745
that I lost in this section.

00:56:41.580 --> 00:56:43.980
So this-- d phi--

00:56:43.980 --> 00:56:45.810
gives me 0.

00:56:45.810 --> 00:56:47.205
So that's my energy balance.

00:56:50.420 --> 00:56:57.020
And if I-- sometimes I also
feel this is not necessary,

00:56:57.020 --> 00:56:59.320
I can just write my--

00:56:59.320 --> 00:57:04.130
here is my heat equation,
heat carried by charge.

00:57:04.130 --> 00:57:09.880
But if I add the electrochemical
energy by charge together,

00:57:09.880 --> 00:57:11.620
that's the total energy--

00:57:11.620 --> 00:57:13.030
energy flux.

00:57:13.030 --> 00:57:16.940
And that will give me
