minus the electronic thermal
conductivity-- dt, dx.

00:58:11.840 --> 00:58:16.220
And in fact, I say I should
erase this electronic.

00:58:16.220 --> 00:58:19.040
I should just use
the total thermal

00:58:19.040 --> 00:58:21.890
because I also have phonons.

00:58:21.890 --> 00:58:25.430
I have the lattice
heat conduction.

00:58:25.430 --> 00:58:28.810
So I combine them all
together-- dt, dx.

00:58:28.810 --> 00:58:34.660
And then dx-- so this is in the
difference of the two over this

00:58:34.660 --> 00:58:36.100
section dx--

00:58:36.100 --> 00:58:40.300
plus I d phi, dx.

00:58:40.300 --> 00:58:42.460
d phi equals 0.

00:58:42.460 --> 00:58:48.220
But recall that I have to,
when I look at the d phi,

00:58:48.220 --> 00:58:53.408
dx, I have to look at
the JQ and dt, dx term.

00:58:53.408 --> 00:58:53.950
It's coupled.

00:58:56.620 --> 00:59:03.310
When I have d ph, dx, I have to
go back and replace d phi, dx

00:59:03.310 --> 00:59:06.010
in terms of JQ and dt, dx.

00:59:06.010 --> 00:59:09.380
I have another term.

00:59:09.380 --> 00:59:14.250
So now you put
all this together.

00:59:14.250 --> 00:59:16.010
I'm not going through the math.

00:59:16.010 --> 00:59:18.890
And what you have is K--

00:59:18.890 --> 00:59:20.840
if I assume temperature--

00:59:20.840 --> 00:59:24.320
say, thermal conductivity
is temperature independent,

00:59:24.320 --> 00:59:26.510
that's the first term-- dt, dx--

00:59:26.510 --> 00:59:28.370
kdx squared.

00:59:28.370 --> 00:59:31.880
That's typically what I have
a heat conduction equation.

00:59:31.880 --> 00:59:33.740
But I have an extra term.

00:59:33.740 --> 00:59:35.450
That's what I want to show.

00:59:35.450 --> 00:59:53.570
This is JQ Tdx, dt, and dt,
dx plus J squared over sigma

00:59:53.570 --> 00:59:54.800
equals 0.

00:59:54.800 --> 01:00:03.700
So this is the Joule
heating is I squared r.

01:00:03.700 --> 01:00:07.210
This is the heat conduction--
normal heat conduction.

01:00:07.210 --> 01:00:11.670
This term is a reversible.

01:00:11.670 --> 01:00:14.630
There is an extra term
when I pass a current

01:00:14.630 --> 01:00:17.270
through the same
material and depends

01:00:17.270 --> 01:00:19.760
on the ratio of this current
and the relative temperature

01:00:19.760 --> 01:00:24.590
gradient I can have along the
material heating and cooling.

01:00:28.320 --> 01:00:30.030
And this is the Thompson term--

01:00:37.080 --> 01:00:38.440
Thompson effect.

01:00:38.440 --> 01:00:44.690
So that's Lord Kelvin,
and he discovered--

01:00:44.690 --> 01:00:49.730
so Seebeck and Peltier were
all experimentally discovered.

01:00:49.730 --> 01:00:51.950
And he tried to connect them.

01:00:51.950 --> 01:00:55.070
And he said, there has
to be a third effect.

01:00:55.070 --> 01:00:58.810
And he was honored for
this Thompson effect.

01:00:58.810 --> 01:01:04.120
And this one is reversible
when you pass current

01:01:04.120 --> 01:01:05.290
through the material.

01:01:05.290 --> 01:01:07.510
And in fact, I say
the negative sign

01:01:07.510 --> 01:01:10.120
because we have here is
more heat generation.

01:01:10.120 --> 01:01:16.900
This is a cooling in the
current rotation there.

01:01:16.900 --> 01:01:18.775
So the Thompson effect--

01:01:23.380 --> 01:01:25.300
for this Thompson
effect, we can also

01:01:25.300 --> 01:01:28.240
define Thompson coefficient.

01:01:28.240 --> 01:01:34.210
So thermoelectric effect-- when
we talk thermoelectric effects,

01:01:34.210 --> 01:01:36.650
there are actually
three effects.

01:01:36.650 --> 01:01:40.630
So Thompson coefficient
is the cooling--

01:01:40.630 --> 01:01:49.690
the local cooling
divided by Jq, dt, dx.

01:01:49.690 --> 01:01:55.600
And this one is T ds, dt.

01:01:55.600 --> 01:01:57.671
And this is Thompson
coefficient.

01:02:04.410 --> 01:02:09.200
So in fact, say, all
thermoelectric coefficient--

01:02:09.200 --> 01:02:12.290
Thomson coefficient,
here, Kelvin relation

01:02:12.290 --> 01:02:14.300
between Peltier and
Seebeck coefficient,

01:02:14.300 --> 01:02:15.755
you see, it's all related.

01:02:18.280 --> 01:02:24.310
And there was a very interesting
guy in thermal electrics.

01:02:24.310 --> 01:02:25.030
He's a Russian.

01:02:25.030 --> 01:02:30.550
And he has a book on various
thermoelectric effects.

01:02:30.550 --> 01:02:32.200
It's dependent on
how you arrange

01:02:32.200 --> 01:02:35.800
your magnetic field, temperature
gradient, current flow--

01:02:35.800 --> 01:02:37.840
there are probably
over 100 effects.

01:02:37.840 --> 01:02:40.177
He has a table there.

01:02:40.177 --> 01:02:42.010
Every time I go to
thermoelectric conference

01:02:42.010 --> 01:02:45.100
we give a talk,
you'll see that table.

01:02:45.100 --> 01:02:46.640
Some of this might
be interesting.

01:02:46.640 --> 01:02:50.260
I really haven't looked
into all those details.

01:02:50.260 --> 01:02:52.930
So the point is, see,
those coupled transport--

01:02:52.930 --> 01:02:54.460
there are many variations.

01:02:57.400 --> 01:03:01.600
So now, you can go
ahead go to solve--

01:03:01.600 --> 01:03:03.310
this is a
differentiable equation.

01:03:03.310 --> 01:03:05.810
If you, typically, your
Seebeck coefficient

01:03:05.810 --> 01:03:07.870
will be, indeed,
temperature-dependent

01:03:07.870 --> 01:03:10.450
so you can go to solve it.

01:03:10.450 --> 01:03:12.700
And the most time,
this coefficient--

01:03:12.700 --> 01:03:15.740
the Thompson effect-- is
actually is not very strong.

01:03:15.740 --> 01:03:20.260
So you can approximately
neglect it.

01:03:20.260 --> 01:03:25.030
So if you neglect it, now we
go to look at a simple device

01:03:25.030 --> 01:03:25.810
analysis.

01:03:33.430 --> 01:03:38.060
So the simple this
device is just a one leg.

01:03:38.060 --> 01:03:40.910
Normally, you never
use one leg because you

01:03:40.910 --> 01:03:42.800
have to put the leads there.

01:03:42.800 --> 01:03:44.930
But when you think
about analysis,

01:03:44.930 --> 01:03:48.020
let's say if I do a cooling--

01:03:48.020 --> 01:03:53.400
if I pass a current through
and say the current--

01:03:53.400 --> 01:03:56.700
let's say this is a
hole positive charge.

01:03:56.700 --> 01:03:59.030
So the current flow
in this direction.

01:03:59.030 --> 01:04:00.620
The hole flow this direction.

01:04:00.620 --> 01:04:03.590
They carry energy from this
side to the other side.

01:04:03.590 --> 01:04:04.700
The side cools down.

01:04:04.700 --> 01:04:06.420
This side heats up.

01:04:06.420 --> 01:04:09.020
So let's say this is
a hot, this is a cold.

01:04:12.750 --> 01:04:15.420
You could create a Peltier.

01:04:15.420 --> 01:04:18.450
That's the current application
of thermal electrics mostly is

01:04:18.450 --> 01:04:20.935
using for cooling.

01:04:23.710 --> 01:04:27.100
So if I neglect the
Thompson effect,

01:04:27.100 --> 01:04:31.610
you go to solve this equation
based on the boundary

01:04:31.610 --> 01:04:34.940
condition, Tc, Th, you can
find the temperature gradient,

01:04:34.940 --> 01:04:36.890
temperature profile
in the material.

01:04:36.890 --> 01:04:43.710
That's easy-- uniform heat
generation, heat conduction.

01:04:43.710 --> 01:04:46.600
If you took a heat transfer
course, you can solve it.

01:04:46.600 --> 01:04:48.820
But if not, but you
can still solve this.

01:04:48.820 --> 01:04:53.250
It's a second-order, very
easy differential equation.

01:04:53.250 --> 01:05:01.350
OK, so if you solve it, you will
find out the heat conduction.

01:05:01.350 --> 01:05:07.550
At the cold surface,
you have conduction.

01:05:07.550 --> 01:05:11.600
And this conduction is because
by cooling, you [INAUDIBLE]

01:05:11.600 --> 01:05:14.120
temperature grid and Th and Tc.

01:05:14.120 --> 01:05:16.670
So you have a reverse
heat flow due to heat

01:05:16.670 --> 01:05:18.570
condition of the material.

01:05:18.570 --> 01:05:23.840
So this heat condition
is kT Th minus Tc.

01:05:23.840 --> 01:05:25.340
And this is coming back.

01:05:25.340 --> 01:05:26.900
This is actually
not what you want.

01:05:31.710 --> 01:05:36.780
But also, there is another term
because I have a Joule heating

01:05:36.780 --> 01:05:39.240
term.

01:05:39.240 --> 01:05:43.180
So this Joule heating--
again, you can prove,

01:05:43.180 --> 01:05:45.700
I say, mathematically.

01:05:45.700 --> 01:05:48.290
Joule heating is the uniform
distributing the lag.

01:05:48.290 --> 01:05:51.740
If you solve that equation,
you find out one half go back.

01:05:51.740 --> 01:05:54.030
The other half goes
the other direction.

01:05:54.030 --> 01:05:58.370
So what you have
is 1/2 I squared--

01:05:58.370 --> 01:05:59.960
I is the current.

01:05:59.960 --> 01:06:02.210
R is the electroresistance.

01:06:02.210 --> 01:06:05.240
So electroresistance
is 1 over sigma,

01:06:05.240 --> 01:06:09.815
L over A. L is the length.

01:06:09.815 --> 01:06:11.105
A is the cross section.

01:06:16.080 --> 01:06:20.090
So this is a conduction due
to the established temporary

01:06:20.090 --> 01:06:23.240
difference reverse flow
and due to the Joule heat,

01:06:23.240 --> 01:06:27.070
conduct back at this interface.

01:06:27.070 --> 01:06:29.730
So this will reverse
heat flow back.

01:06:29.730 --> 01:06:32.730
And now if you make a cooler,
your electrical current

01:06:32.730 --> 01:06:33.940
is flow this direction.

01:06:33.940 --> 01:06:37.990
They carry a heat away
from this interface.

01:06:37.990 --> 01:06:40.050
So the left cooling--

01:06:40.050 --> 01:06:47.370
Q cooling is the Peltier
coefficient times current,

01:06:47.370 --> 01:06:49.830
which Peltier is TS--

01:06:49.830 --> 01:06:52.320
Thompson-- times current.

01:06:52.320 --> 01:06:57.180
And then counterbalanced
by this reversed heat flow,

01:06:57.180 --> 01:07:01.200
so let's say Kth.

01:07:01.200 --> 01:07:04.230
K-- this is a conductance--

01:07:04.230 --> 01:07:14.300
Kth Th minus Tc minus
1/2 I squared Re.

01:07:14.300 --> 01:07:17.090
So this is the left
cooling you have.

01:07:17.090 --> 01:07:20.030
What you really
want is this term.

01:07:20.030 --> 01:07:23.180
But because of the material has
a finite thermal conductivity,

01:07:23.180 --> 01:07:24.500
you want a 0.

01:07:24.500 --> 01:07:27.150
That's good.

01:07:27.150 --> 01:07:30.330
So you have heat reverse back.

01:07:30.330 --> 01:07:33.970
You have Joule heating
in the material.

01:07:33.970 --> 01:07:37.210
So you also don't like that.

01:07:37.210 --> 01:07:38.610
You want the S large.

01:07:41.870 --> 01:07:44.780
But you look at
this, the cooling--

01:07:44.780 --> 01:07:47.960
Peltier cooling is linear
proportional to current.

01:07:47.960 --> 01:07:51.620
Joule heating is I squared, so
there is an optimum current.

01:07:51.620 --> 01:07:54.110
You can take that.

01:07:54.110 --> 01:08:00.380
You find that I optimum, then
you take the gradient equal 0,

01:08:00.380 --> 01:08:02.030
derivative equal 0.

01:08:02.030 --> 01:08:07.880
Then the I optimum
is STc over Re.

01:08:07.880 --> 01:08:14.930
So that's the optimum
current for the cooling.

01:08:14.930 --> 01:08:17.660
And in this case, you'll
have the maximum cooling.

01:08:20.700 --> 01:08:22.649
The maximum cooling--

01:08:22.649 --> 01:08:24.490
I'm not going to write it down.

01:08:24.490 --> 01:08:31.189
But when the maximum cooling is
0, you say, why do you do that?

01:08:31.189 --> 01:08:33.499
When it's 0, you actually
create the largest delta

01:08:33.499 --> 01:08:38.270
T. You don't cool anything, your
maximum delta T you can have.

01:08:38.270 --> 01:08:39.800
So the maximum delta T--

01:08:39.800 --> 01:08:41.460
I'm going to write it down.

01:08:41.460 --> 01:08:49.520
So when you have also
Q cooling equals 0,

01:08:49.520 --> 01:08:56.240
then you get your maximum
T hot minus T cold maximum.

01:08:56.240 --> 01:08:59.399
And it turns out, this
is a quadratic equation.

01:08:59.399 --> 01:09:03.680
So 1/2 ZTc squared.

01:09:03.680 --> 01:09:15.310
And this Z is a famous ZT figure
of merit of the material where

01:09:15.310 --> 01:09:21.460
Z including S
squared sigma over K.

01:09:21.460 --> 01:09:23.229
And this is the figure of merit.

01:09:35.640 --> 01:09:38.819
So the unit of this
is the inverse Kelvin.

01:09:38.819 --> 01:09:40.890
You go the check.

01:09:40.890 --> 01:09:44.279
And most of the time, it
appears as a product of Z

01:09:44.279 --> 01:09:46.029
with the temperature.

01:09:46.029 --> 01:09:49.529
So ZT is a long dimension.

01:09:49.529 --> 01:09:50.370
Oh, OK.

01:09:50.370 --> 01:09:52.689
This is a cooling.

01:09:52.689 --> 01:09:55.500
But say, for cooling, I
don't necessarily want--

01:09:55.500 --> 01:09:57.990
I want what's the
most efficient, right?

01:09:57.990 --> 01:10:00.530
I also care about the
efficiency of cooling.

01:10:00.530 --> 01:10:03.600
If I make a refrigerator
and that's a--

01:10:03.600 --> 01:10:06.450
say, you don't coefficient
when you make a refrigerator.

01:10:06.450 --> 01:10:09.647
You say, what do
you pay electricity,

01:10:09.647 --> 01:10:10.980
and what do you gain in cooling?

01:10:10.980 --> 01:10:12.930
That coefficient of performance.

01:10:12.930 --> 01:10:22.050
So COP of cooling
is Q of cooling

01:10:22.050 --> 01:10:27.310
and how much you pay
in your electric bill--

01:10:27.310 --> 01:10:27.810
the power.

01:10:30.840 --> 01:10:33.910
And the cooling I already have.

01:10:33.910 --> 01:10:41.060
So what I have here, this one
you can say is a function of--

01:10:41.060 --> 01:10:42.090
let me just--

01:10:42.090 --> 01:10:47.700
I'm not going to copy it, but
you can put this one there.

01:10:47.700 --> 01:10:50.940
But what's interesting is
what, in this case, what

01:10:50.940 --> 01:10:54.220
is the electrical power
you have to put in?

01:10:57.690 --> 01:11:01.710
Normally, you would
say, I squared R.

01:11:01.710 --> 01:11:04.980
But that's not the case
for thermoelectrics.

01:11:04.980 --> 01:11:06.690
You will have I square Re.

01:11:13.450 --> 01:11:17.270
That's the resistance--
internal resistance.

01:11:17.270 --> 01:11:24.770
But you also produce,
here, a Seebeck voltage.

01:11:24.770 --> 01:11:30.160
It's S Th minus Tc, right?

01:11:30.160 --> 01:11:34.550
So if you think about that, I'm
drawing is a positive Seebeck.

01:11:34.550 --> 01:11:37.660
So the Seebeck voltage
is actually reversed

01:11:37.660 --> 01:11:40.710
because you have more--

01:11:40.710 --> 01:11:41.560
wait.

01:11:41.560 --> 01:11:47.460
OK, so you have more
charge in this direction

01:11:47.460 --> 01:11:48.540
then this direction.

01:11:48.540 --> 01:11:50.920
So you have the--

01:11:54.660 --> 01:11:56.730
what I want to say is
what should be this,

01:11:56.730 --> 01:11:58.920
and sometimes I'm
getting confused, now.

01:11:58.920 --> 01:12:00.810
So the sign should be--

01:12:00.810 --> 01:12:03.900
because I'm only using
one type of material.

01:12:03.900 --> 01:12:09.620
But if it's-- basically, I
need to put in more power.

01:12:09.620 --> 01:12:14.790
The generated Seebeck voltage
resist my current flow.

01:12:14.790 --> 01:12:20.930
So it's S Th minus
Tc times I. So

01:12:20.930 --> 01:12:26.190
this is additional voltage
generally due to the current--

01:12:26.190 --> 01:12:27.900
due to the
temperature difference

01:12:27.900 --> 01:12:31.130
exist across a device.

01:12:31.130 --> 01:12:36.200
OK, so I'm paying more to
overcome that temperature

01:12:36.200 --> 01:12:37.870
difference.

01:12:37.870 --> 01:12:41.430
So because of this, you can
see, when I'm optimizing my COP,

01:12:41.430 --> 01:12:45.660
I have to take the derivative
of this with respect to current.

01:12:45.660 --> 01:12:49.290
That's different than I
take a derivative of Q

01:12:49.290 --> 01:12:51.060
with respect to current.

01:12:51.060 --> 01:12:56.190
I have a different optimum
point for maximum efficiency

01:12:56.190 --> 01:12:57.900
versus maximum cooling power.

01:12:57.900 --> 01:13:02.390
Those are the operation
of the device.

01:13:02.390 --> 01:13:05.090
OK, so you optimize this.

01:13:05.090 --> 01:13:07.320
What do you find out?

01:13:07.320 --> 01:13:10.130
If you maximize--
so this is like when

01:13:10.130 --> 01:13:12.800
you operate your solar sail.

01:13:12.800 --> 01:13:19.000
There is always an optimum point
because you don't want to run,

01:13:19.000 --> 01:13:21.200
say, a solar sail--
later on, you'll learn--

01:13:21.200 --> 01:13:24.170
this is the solar sail
typically current voltage.

01:13:24.170 --> 01:13:28.970
And you want to say
this is your current--

01:13:28.970 --> 01:13:30.350
this is a voltage.

01:13:30.350 --> 01:13:31.550
This is a current.

01:13:31.550 --> 01:13:35.030
And you want to optimize so that
the current times the voltage

01:13:35.030 --> 01:13:37.840
is a maximum.

01:13:37.840 --> 01:13:40.270
So those kind of
solid-state device,

01:13:40.270 --> 01:13:42.820
there is always optimal loading.

01:13:42.820 --> 01:13:47.480
You match the external
load to the internal load.

01:13:47.480 --> 01:13:55.190
And when I do that, my optimum,
say, COP is Tc, Th minus Tc.

01:13:55.190 --> 01:13:56.600
This is a Carnot factor.

01:13:56.600 --> 01:14:00.130
If you have a Carnot
engine, that's what you get.

01:14:00.130 --> 01:14:08.530
And then, 1 plus ZTm minus
the Th over Tc and square

01:14:08.530 --> 01:14:14.160
root 1 plus ZTm minus 1--

01:14:14.160 --> 01:14:17.450
oh, plus 1.

01:14:17.450 --> 01:14:22.080
OK, so this is a long
idea factor here.

01:14:22.080 --> 01:14:28.770
ZTm-- Tm is the
average of Th minus Tc.

01:14:28.770 --> 01:14:33.840
So you can see, I always get
the product of Z times T.

01:14:33.840 --> 01:14:38.000
That's where the ZT come from.

01:14:38.000 --> 01:14:41.660
OK, here is a Carnot factor.

01:14:41.660 --> 01:14:45.650
Here is a long idea
material factor.

01:14:45.650 --> 01:14:48.080
And you want to improve
this material ZT,

01:14:48.080 --> 01:14:52.840
so you increase those.

01:14:52.840 --> 01:14:56.500
And you will see, this is
probably about 1/5 in many--

01:14:56.500 --> 01:14:59.980
given ZT equals 1, that's
a lot of material--

01:14:59.980 --> 01:15:01.120
Carnot material.

01:15:01.120 --> 01:15:05.600
And that factor-- long
idea factor-- is 1/5.

01:15:05.600 --> 01:15:07.580
So it depends, of course,
that depends on how

01:15:07.580 --> 01:15:10.070
large is your Th minus Tc.

01:15:10.070 --> 01:15:12.627
Just can't reach certain-- if
your Th over Tc is too large,

01:15:12.627 --> 01:15:13.460
you use [INAUDIBLE].

01:15:13.460 --> 01:15:16.220
You don't make a
refrigerator out of it.

01:15:16.220 --> 01:15:19.220
You have maximum delta
T that you can achieve.

01:15:23.610 --> 01:15:26.820
And you can also make
a power generator.

01:15:26.820 --> 01:15:31.180
You reverse it, you'll
make a power generator.

01:15:31.180 --> 01:15:36.240
And that's the-- again, if
you make a power generator,

01:15:36.240 --> 01:15:38.900
you have a maximum,
say, efficiency.

01:15:42.570 --> 01:15:45.310
And you need to
[INAUDIBLE] match it.

01:15:45.310 --> 01:15:57.780
So in the case of
power generator,

01:15:57.780 --> 01:16:03.300
your maximal efficiency
if you do it is--

01:16:03.300 --> 01:16:08.490
this is, again, Carnot factor
if you operate the heat

01:16:08.490 --> 01:16:12.480
engine between a Tc and Th.

01:16:12.480 --> 01:16:14.850
And the imperfection
due to material

01:16:14.850 --> 01:16:26.520
is 1ZTm minus 1, 1 plus
ZTm plus Tc over Th.

01:16:26.520 --> 01:16:30.560
So this is everything why,
in the thermoelectrics,

01:16:30.560 --> 01:16:33.430
that people will
say, what's your ZT?

01:16:33.430 --> 01:16:37.120
Because you can't do much
with the Carnot factor.

01:16:37.120 --> 01:16:38.470
Of course, you can always--

01:16:38.470 --> 01:16:40.630
depend on heat
source, temperature

01:16:40.630 --> 01:16:44.470
range-- you can improve this
one, higher temperature.

01:16:44.470 --> 01:16:48.640
But it's the material,
also, how much you

01:16:48.640 --> 01:16:50.140
can reach compared to Carnot.

01:16:55.431 --> 01:16:58.440
And a few more comments--

01:16:58.440 --> 01:17:02.780
first, if you do
thermoelectric material,

01:17:02.780 --> 01:17:06.870
even if you get your best
thermoelectric material

01:17:06.870 --> 01:17:09.540
at hand, you may not know it.

01:17:09.540 --> 01:17:16.030
Because thermoelectric
material is a three parameter.

01:17:16.030 --> 01:17:17.380
And this three parameter--

01:17:17.380 --> 01:17:24.970
ZT-- if we look at ZT
electron in the numerator,

01:17:24.970 --> 01:17:27.790
thermal conductivity is the
electronic thermal conductivity

01:17:27.790 --> 01:17:30.040
plus phonon thermal
conductivity.

01:17:30.040 --> 01:17:32.090
You have both.

01:17:32.090 --> 01:17:37.270
K is Ke plus K phonon.

01:17:37.270 --> 01:17:42.310
OK, so we said that in
thermoelectric, this Fermi

01:17:42.310 --> 01:17:46.180
level can be
controlled by doping.

01:17:46.180 --> 01:17:50.080
So it turns out, the
electric conductivity,

01:17:50.080 --> 01:17:54.860
Seebeck coefficient
all depends on doping.

01:17:54.860 --> 01:17:58.920
And if you dope the, say, very
high, you get in the metals.

01:17:58.920 --> 01:18:00.390
Seebeck cancel each other.

01:18:00.390 --> 01:18:04.650
We comment before, Seebeck
in metal is very low.

01:18:04.650 --> 01:18:12.620
So Seebeck drop as you increase
your carrier concentration.

01:18:12.620 --> 01:18:14.870
On the other hand, the
electric conductivity,

01:18:14.870 --> 01:18:16.820
if you look at--
it's mobility times

01:18:16.820 --> 01:18:20.010
carrier concentration
times charge.

01:18:20.010 --> 01:18:24.070
So electric conductivity
will increase--

01:18:24.070 --> 01:18:29.250
right sigma S. So you have
to optimize S square sigma.

01:18:29.250 --> 01:18:32.270
You need to maximize.

01:18:32.270 --> 01:18:34.340
And in fact, even
this maximum point

01:18:34.340 --> 01:18:38.390
may not be the maximum
because your Ke depends

01:18:38.390 --> 01:18:42.270
on carrier concentration.

01:18:42.270 --> 01:18:50.850
So your Ke is normally small,
but this is Ke typical.

01:18:50.850 --> 01:18:54.150
Phonon-- which is the quality
independent on carrier

01:18:54.150 --> 01:18:54.900
concentration.

01:18:54.900 --> 01:18:57.360
Carrier can scatter phonon.

01:18:57.360 --> 01:18:59.880
Once you get a very high
carrier concentration,

01:18:59.880 --> 01:19:06.680
Wiedeman-Franz law will say
it's proportional-- sigma K.

01:19:06.680 --> 01:19:09.980
So that gives you
a typical ZT curve.

01:19:09.980 --> 01:19:13.700
This is your ZT, and this
is-- your material is about 10

01:19:13.700 --> 01:19:14.460
to the 19.

01:19:14.460 --> 01:19:16.880
It could be 10 to the
17 to 10 to the 21.

01:19:16.880 --> 01:19:18.360
This is the range.

01:19:18.360 --> 01:19:20.090
So that's what I say--

01:19:20.090 --> 01:19:24.020
this is 1 over centimeter cubic.

01:19:24.020 --> 01:19:26.420
So even if you have
the best material,

01:19:26.420 --> 01:19:30.710
may not know it because your
[INAUDIBLE] is not right,

01:19:30.710 --> 01:19:32.195
so it just doesn't work.

01:19:32.195 --> 01:19:34.070
So that's the difficulty
with thermoelectric.

01:19:34.070 --> 01:19:35.030
You have to control.

01:19:35.030 --> 01:19:36.800
You have to search.

01:19:36.800 --> 01:19:40.670
You change one parameter,
the other parameter change.

01:19:43.550 --> 01:19:46.850
Sometimes people-- this
is a control properties.

01:19:46.850 --> 01:19:49.520
Say, one way, the other way.

01:19:49.520 --> 01:19:52.310
They say, how you can be--
how you can fool the material,

01:19:52.310 --> 01:19:56.400
how you can fool nature
to get better materials?

01:19:56.400 --> 01:20:00.140
Second comment is this ZT--

01:20:00.140 --> 01:20:03.440
Z seems like analysis is
temperature-independent,

01:20:03.440 --> 01:20:05.640
but it's never
temperature-independent.

01:20:05.640 --> 01:20:07.670
It's always
temperature-dependent.

01:20:07.670 --> 01:20:12.560
So you go to look at your
temperature dependence.

01:20:12.560 --> 01:20:15.800
This is temperature.

01:20:15.800 --> 01:20:19.384
And Seebeck typically
increase with temperature.

01:20:21.990 --> 01:20:24.750
Why is that?

01:20:24.750 --> 01:20:28.400
So when I look at
the semiconductor,

01:20:28.400 --> 01:20:31.340
I increase the temperature.

01:20:31.340 --> 01:20:34.040
The Fermi level moved to
the center of the band.

01:20:34.040 --> 01:20:37.070
So Ef actually
goes to the center.

01:20:37.070 --> 01:20:42.650
I'm changing the average
energy of Ef and electron.

01:20:42.650 --> 01:20:44.510
So that energy difference
becomes larger.

01:20:47.510 --> 01:20:49.970
But they only increase
with a certain level,

01:20:49.970 --> 01:20:51.095
then you start to drop.

01:20:54.000 --> 01:20:56.680
Why is that?

01:20:56.680 --> 01:21:00.120
That's because when I go
to very high temperature,

01:21:00.120 --> 01:21:01.950
this is at the center.

01:21:01.950 --> 01:21:04.740
There are equal
number electron holes.

01:21:04.740 --> 01:21:06.390
The hole carry negative charge.

01:21:06.390 --> 01:21:09.390
They cancel the positive charge.

01:21:09.390 --> 01:21:11.040
So even though they
can't contribute

01:21:11.040 --> 01:21:14.330
to electric conductivity both
because you have electron hole,

01:21:14.330 --> 01:21:16.220
the energy that carries
is the opposite,

01:21:16.220 --> 01:21:18.090
and they cancel each other.

01:21:18.090 --> 01:21:19.265
So this is a bipolar.

01:21:25.490 --> 01:21:28.340
And the bipolar is really bad.

01:21:28.340 --> 01:21:32.600
So what happens, the electrical
is typically dropped and this--

01:21:32.600 --> 01:21:35.190
they may not get at the same
point-- same temperature.

01:21:35.190 --> 01:21:38.700
But those are different
signs of bipolar.

01:21:38.700 --> 01:21:43.050
And also interesting-- it's bad
is because the thermal is also

01:21:43.050 --> 01:21:46.040
shooting up at the bipolar.

01:21:46.040 --> 01:21:49.580
That's because when bipolar
[INAUDIBLE],, the electron hole

01:21:49.580 --> 01:21:51.510
diffuse from one
side to the other,

01:21:51.510 --> 01:21:54.680
they actually carry
the bandgap energy.

01:21:54.680 --> 01:22:00.680
So they carry a lot of heat, so
thermal conductivity is large.

01:22:00.680 --> 01:22:04.040
So the ZT will peak at
a certain temperature.

01:22:04.040 --> 01:22:05.200
This is ZT.

01:22:05.200 --> 01:22:08.740
It will peak at
certain temperature.

01:22:08.740 --> 01:22:11.310
So for different
applications, you

01:22:11.310 --> 01:22:14.040
optimize and find the material
for different temperature

01:22:14.040 --> 01:22:16.470
range.

01:22:16.470 --> 01:22:21.320
So those are the challenges
to thermoelectrics.

01:22:21.320 --> 01:22:24.280
OK, I'll stop here.

